    "content": "Released on May 2, 2006, Tool's \"10,000 Days\" arrived five years after \"Lateralus\" and thirteen years before \"Fear Inoculum,\" occupying a pivotal place in Danny Carey's evolution as a drummer. Debuting at #1 on the Billboard 200 and reaching the top spot in nine countries, the album captured Tool at the height of their commercial power while pushing their sonic ambitions further than ever before.\n\nThe album's title honors Maynard James Keenan's mother, Judith Marie, who spent approximately 10,000 days in a wheelchair following a stroke — lending a raw emotional weight to the record. For Danny, that emotional depth demanded drumming that was simultaneously technically complex and deeply human.\n\n\"10,000 Days\" won the Grammy Award for Best Recording Package at the 49th Grammy Awards in 2007, recognizing the album's extraordinary visual and physical presentation. The gatefold package included lenticular 3D glasses — an artistic statement entirely in keeping with Tool's reputation for immersive, concept-driven releases.\n\nDanny's drumming on this album represents the zenith of his Lateralus-era setup, refined over five years of touring and studio exploration. Tracks like \"Vicarious\" (7/8), \"Jambi\" (complex polyrhythm over a sustained drone), and the epic title suite \"10,000 Days (Wings Pt. 1 & 2)\" pushed his odd-time mastery to new heights. This article breaks down every piece of gear Danny used to create this landmark recording.",
