    "content": "\"10,000 Days\" was recorded primarily at Cello Studios (now EastWest) in Hollywood and The Hook in North Hollywood — the same facilities used for Lateralus. Tool's decision to return to familiar studios reflected a desire for continuity and the comfort of known acoustic environments.\n\n**Self-Production:**\nFollowing the successful self-production approach, Tool handled the majority of production duties themselves with mixing by Joe Barresi. This gave Danny the freedom to experiment extensively — the Roland SPD-S integration required careful setup, and self-production allowed unlimited time to dial in the electronic elements alongside the acoustic setup.\n\n**Microphone Setup:**\n- Kick drum: AKG D112 inside, Neumann FET 47 outside for the subsonic depth\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top, AKG C451 bottom for crack definition\n- Toms: Sennheiser MD421 on each tom, preserving the Heavy Beech's warmth\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451 for clear pattern articulation\n- Overheads: Neumann U87 stereo pair for the full kit image\n- Room mics: Multiple Neumann U47s at varying distances\n- SPD-S: Direct signal routed through Pro Tools for mixing flexibility\n\n**The 7/8 Challenge:**\nRecording \"Vicarious\" required particular focus on the 7/8 groove. Danny recorded without a click track — his internal pulse drove the tempo. This organic approach preserved the subtle push-and-pull that gives the song its hypnotic quality. The snare's bronze warmth sat perfectly in the mid-frequency space that Adam Jones's guitar left open.\n\n**Jambi's Drone Integration:**\n\"Jambi\" presented unique challenges. The song opens with a whammy pedal drone, and Danny needed to establish his polyrhythmic pattern against a non-standard reference pitch. His drumming locks into the drone rather than fighting it — a testament to his musical ear and his kit's tonal relationship with the guitars.",
